    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's denote the two numbers as \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), with \\(x > y\\). According to the problem, we have two equations:\n1. \\(x + y = 6\\)\n2. \\(x^2 - y^2 = 12\\)\n\nWe know the algebraic identity \\(x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y)\\). Using this identity, we can rewrite the second equation as:\n\\[ (x + y)(x - y) = 12 \\]\nSubstituting \\(x + y = 6\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[ 6(x - y) = 12 \\]\nDividing both sides by 6, we find:\n\\[ x - y = 2 \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\(x + y = 6\\)\n2. \\(x - y = 2\\)\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations:\n\\[ (x + y) + (x - y) = 6 + 2 \\]\n\\[ 2x = 8 \\]\n\\[ x = 4 \\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(x = 4\\) back into the first equation:\n\\[ 4 + y = 6 \\]\n\\[ y = 2 \\]\n\nSo, the two numbers are \\(4\\) and \\(2\\). The positive difference between these two numbers is:\n\\[ x - y = 4 - 2 = 2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
